# The prelude

A package's **prelude** is the file `prelude.hql` at its module root: a list
of `import` statements that every unit of a run imports while the package
is active. It is not a module: it has no name and cannot be imported. The
standard library has one, `lib/std/prelude.hql`, which is why a program
writes `trim` and `map` with no import; a package's own works the same way
for the package's modules.

`hello`'s prelude is one line:

```hql
import hello
```

## Loaded and active

Two states of a package are distinct:

| State | Holds when | Effect |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **loaded** | the package is discovered and its library opens | its modules can be imported |
| **active** | a unit of the run imports one of its modules | its prelude's imports are added to every unit |

Every discovered package is loaded when `hql` starts. A loaded package
that no unit imports adds no name to any program:

<!-- run: --project examples/packages -->
```hql
hello.greet("Ada")
```

```text
check error[unknown-name]: `hello` is not bound
 --> <expression>:1:1
  |
1 | hello.greet("Ada")
  | ^^^^^
```

An import of one of the package's modules activates it. Here the import
binds the module under the alias `h`, and the prelude binds it under its
own name:

<!-- run: --project examples/packages -->
```hql
import hello as h

[h.greet("Ada"), hello.greet("Bo")]
```

```text
["Hello, Ada!", "Hello, Bo!"]
```

## What activates a package

A package is active in a run when one of its modules is imported by any of
these:

- an `import` in the program;
- an `import` in a project module the program reaches through its imports;
- `[modules] import` in a configuration file, naming a module or the
  package;
- `--import <module>` on the command line.

Activation is per run, not per unit. The units of a run are the program
and every project module it reaches. An import in one of them activates
the package for all of them, so a project module that writes no import of
the package still reads the names its prelude binds once another unit has
imported it.

## What a prelude is for

A prelude names the modules a program of the package cannot do without. A
module it does not name is the package's optional part and is imported
where it is used. The `ml` package ([[38-the-ml-package|chapter 38]])
provides `ml` and `ml.metrics`, and its prelude is:

```hql
import ml
```

A program that imports only `ml.metrics` activates the package, so `ml`
is bound too, and `Dataset` and `fit_linear` are in scope. `ml.metrics`
stays out of a program that does not import it.

## What a prelude may hold

- Each statement MUST be an `import` or a `from` statement. A declaration,
  a binding or an expression refuses the package.
- An import MUST NOT be written with `as`: a prelude imports a module
  under its own name, its last segment.
- Each module named MUST exist: a module of the package, or of a builtin
  package.

A package whose prelude breaks one of these is refused when it is loaded,
and an `import` of one of its modules reports the reason:

```text
check error[unknown-name]: no module named `hello`: .../modules/prelude.hql: a prelude holds imports and nothing else
```

Two active packages whose preludes import different modules under one
last segment are an error of the run, naming both files. A prelude's
import that collides with a name the program binds itself is resolved in
the program, by importing under another name with `as`.

## Declining preludes

`prelude = false` under `[modules]` in `.hql/config.toml` withholds every
prelude's imports, `std`'s and each active package's:

```toml
[modules]
prelude = false
```

Every unit of the project then imports each module it uses. The setting
does not change which packages are loaded or active.

## The prelude does not apply inside its package

A contract under the package's module root imports every module whose
names it writes, `core` included ([The contract](contracts.hmd)). The
package's prelude reaches the units of a run, the program and project
modules, and no module of a package.
